# The smallest interval with one-limb bounds that contains a ball with a one-limb center
# (milestone2.md, "One enclosure interface", "Midpoint, radius and the conversions", "Ball to
# interval"): [RD(c - r), RU(c + r)]. The whole line (0, +inf) gives [-inf, +inf] by the same two
# operations (0 - inf rounded down is -inf); the empty set (all NaN) is selected, by the test of the
# radius. These are the values of lower() and upper() of a Ball<T, 1>.
kernel:        ball_to_interval_1
input:         stored c
input:         stored r
output:        zl
output:        zu
requires:      ball c r
ensures:       interval_of_ball zl zu x x=c,r tight=rounded
min_precision: 2
source:        the directed difference and sum of the center and the radius (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        unverified
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   unverified
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the bounds are directed sums, and the empty set is selected after an explicit test; interval_of_ball states what comes out for every canonical ball

l  = sub_down_1(c, r)
h  = add_up_1(c, r)
ne = eq(r, r)
mi = neg(const inf)
zl = select(ne, l, const inf)
zu = select(ne, h, mi)
